1
00:00:07,424 --> 00:00:09,175
There's a certain very special quality

2
00:00:09,259 --> 00:00:11,428
that great comedians,
great performers have.

3
00:00:12,011 --> 00:00:13,680
It's a pure survival instinct.

4
00:00:14,055 --> 00:00:15,432
Certain cars have it, too.

5
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:17,767
When even the Police Department says,

6
00:00:17,851 --> 00:00:21,146
"We can't get another mile
out of this thing.

7
00:00:21,938 --> 00:00:24,065
But it won't stop. It can't.

8
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:26,860
It just doesn't know how to die."

9
00:00:26,943 --> 00:00:28,695
[tires squeal, crash]

10
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:31,781
This is one of those cars.

11
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:39,330
It's a 1976 Dodge Royal Monaco sedan.

12
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:41,666
Does anyone need it? No.

13
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:43,418
What is it good for? Nothing.

14
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:48,882
But I like things that exist just because
they decided they want to exist,

15
00:00:48,965 --> 00:00:50,467
which is why I think

16
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:53,470
it's the perfect car
for my very special guest today,

17
00:00:53,553 --> 00:00:55,013
Mr. Seth Rogen.

18
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:58,433
Seth doesn't look like a movie star,
but he is one.

19
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:01,936
He looks like a stand-up comic,
but he isn't one.

20
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,062
It's 'cause I'm Canadian.

21
00:01:03,146 --> 00:01:04,481
Oh.

22
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:06,191
-[phone rings]
-[Seth] Hello?

23
00:01:06,274 --> 00:01:08,485
[Jerry] Seth? Jerry Seinfeld. How are you?

24
00:01:08,568 --> 00:01:10,612
[Seth] I'm wonderful.
How's it going, Jerry?

25
00:01:10,695 --> 00:01:12,739
[Jerry] I like to say,
"I feel fantastic." [laughs]

26
00:01:12,822 --> 00:01:15,992
You're wonderful, I'm fantastic.
We're both lying. Let's do it anyway.

27
00:01:16,076 --> 00:01:18,244
-[Seth] Okay, good.
-[Jerry] All right, see you in a bit.

28
00:01:18,328 --> 00:01:22,457
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

29
00:01:24,292 --> 00:01:25,835
-What's happening, sir?
-[Jerry] Dude.

30
00:01:25,919 --> 00:01:26,753
Good to see you.

31
00:01:26,836 --> 00:01:28,088
-Welcome.
 -Perfect.

32
00:01:28,171 --> 00:01:29,172
Yes. [laughs]

33
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:30,131
It's perfect.

34
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:33,176
-So, it's you and Even Goldberg?
-Me and Evan Goldberg.

35
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:34,844
We're building a better office.

36
00:01:35,136 --> 00:01:36,930
-Where?
-We're leaving the lot altogether.

37
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:39,724
-We almost destroyed this studio.
-Is it 'cause of the Korea thing?

38
00:01:39,808 --> 00:01:44,062
Yeah, it's because we haven't felt
welcome here ever since then. [laughs]

39
00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:47,732
So, when you and Evan write,
where do you sit?

40
00:01:47,816 --> 00:01:49,317
[Seth] We share a computer.

41
00:01:49,776 --> 00:01:52,403
We literally, like,
pass the keyboard back and forth.

42
00:01:52,487 --> 00:01:53,530
[Jerry] How many years?

43
00:01:53,613 --> 00:01:56,032
-We started writing Superbad...
-Right.

44
00:01:56,116 --> 00:01:57,408
...when we were 13 years old.

45
00:01:57,492 --> 00:01:59,702
And now we're 36.

46
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:01,496
We have a good dynamic.
He just had a baby.

47
00:02:01,579 --> 00:02:03,873
-So, he's not here.
-Right. Do you have a baby?

48
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:04,958
I have no children.

49
00:02:05,041 --> 00:02:06,042
-No children?
-No.

50
00:02:06,125 --> 00:02:07,502
-Do you have kids?
-Three.

51
00:02:07,585 --> 00:02:08,419
Three?

52
00:02:08,503 --> 00:02:10,672
Do you regret--
Like, do you like your kids?

53
00:02:11,005 --> 00:02:12,674
-What?
-I'm always wondering...

54
00:02:12,757 --> 00:02:15,885
I'm always looking for someone to admit
they regret having children.

55
00:02:18,805 --> 00:02:19,931
Oh, my God!

56
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:22,475
This is crazy.

57
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:23,977
[both laugh]

58
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:25,436
-It's a cop car.
-Yeah.

59
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,606
-It's kinda like The Blues Brothers' car.
-It's kinda like that, yeah.

60
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:29,607
This is amazing.

61
00:02:30,024 --> 00:02:32,610
-Are you in the mood for coffee?
-I'm always in the mood for coffee.

62
00:02:32,694 --> 00:02:33,528
Me too.

63
00:02:39,117 --> 00:02:41,578
Does your company make other things
other than this show?

64
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:42,871
-No.
-Or do you just do this?

65
00:02:42,954 --> 00:02:44,497
I don't even think my company does this.

66
00:02:44,581 --> 00:02:47,292
-You don't even do that.
-I don't even know if I have a company.

67
00:02:48,668 --> 00:02:51,171
[Jerry] So, how many things
do you have going right now?

68
00:02:51,713 --> 00:02:55,675
Man, we'll have, uh,
four TV shows on the air.

69
00:02:55,758 --> 00:02:57,093
-Wow.
-Yeah.

70
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:59,762
Then we'll have a few movies
coming out in theaters next year.

71
00:02:59,846 --> 00:03:01,139
-Like, big movies.
-Wow.

72
00:03:01,222 --> 00:03:03,433
-Yeah. It's nice.
-Jeez, that's impressive.

73
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:05,810
-Did you ever want to make a movie?
-No.

74
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:10,982
When I moved to LA, I thought
I was gonna be a stand-up comic.

75
00:03:11,065 --> 00:03:12,358
-Right. Wow.
-Like...

76
00:03:12,442 --> 00:03:15,153
And then I started doing
stand-up comedy in LA,

77
00:03:15,236 --> 00:03:19,115
and I was just like, "Oh, I am not nearly
as good as these people." Like...

78
00:03:19,198 --> 00:03:23,077
There's a joke that a comic I know
in Vancouver would tell

79
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:25,747
that I think of all the time,
where he's like, uh,

80
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:29,000
"I wanted to be a boxer until I fought
a guy who really wanted to be a boxer."

81
00:03:29,083 --> 00:03:29,918
Uh-huh.

82
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:32,962
And that's, like, exactly how I felt
about stand-up comedy when I got here.

83
00:03:33,046 --> 00:03:34,714
I was like, "I wanted to be
a stand-up comic

84
00:03:34,797 --> 00:03:37,967
until I was around people
who really wanted to be stand-up comics."

85
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:39,469
-That is fantastic.
-And were like...

86
00:03:41,471 --> 00:03:44,224
Would you rather hurt someone's feelings

87
00:03:44,307 --> 00:03:46,476
or have someone hurt your feelings?

88
00:03:46,559 --> 00:03:49,854
Uh, I probably rather have someone
hurt my feelings.

89
00:03:49,938 --> 00:03:52,273
I don't ever want to hurt
other people's feelings. Obviously.

90
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:54,901
-No, of course not. But we do.
-But it happens. We for sure do.

91
00:03:54,984 --> 00:03:56,527
I don't view

92
00:03:56,611 --> 00:04:00,365
understanding the things
people might be offended by as...

93
00:04:01,115 --> 00:04:03,576
as... as closing in my parameter.

94
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:05,578
-That's right.
-I view it as...

95
00:04:05,662 --> 00:04:08,289
lines that I understand they exist...

96
00:04:08,373 --> 00:04:11,292
-Right. That's right.
-...and I choose to deal with that

97
00:04:11,376 --> 00:04:12,835
in a case-by-case basis.

98
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:14,504
-That's a formula for success.
-Yeah.

99
00:04:14,587 --> 00:04:16,047
But the... but the point

100
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:17,757
-for people like us...
-Yes.

101
00:04:17,882 --> 00:04:21,719
...is to find the line,
and then dance on the line.

102
00:04:21,803 --> 00:04:23,554
-Which I think that we do.
-Right.

103
00:04:23,638 --> 00:04:26,557
I think obliviousness is the thing
that people don't like.

104
00:04:26,641 --> 00:04:28,434
When they don't think you can tell

105
00:04:28,518 --> 00:04:30,687
you're making a joke
that is inappropriate.

106
00:04:30,770 --> 00:04:34,482
If you know it's something
that is taboo to be saying,

107
00:04:34,816 --> 00:04:37,110
and that's folded into the recipe,

108
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:39,612
then you can say
whatever the [bleep] you want.

109
00:04:39,696 --> 00:04:42,824
People don't like it when they feel like
they're in a room with someone

110
00:04:42,907 --> 00:04:44,033
who doesn't understand

111
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:47,036
-where social norms lie.
-Right. Yes. Right, right.

112
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,705
"This guy get where the line is?

113
00:04:48,788 --> 00:04:51,791
Is he gonna say something that, like,
is gonna be super [bleep] weird?"

114
00:04:51,874 --> 00:04:52,709
Yeah.

115
00:04:53,918 --> 00:04:55,420
-[Seth] Are we going to Canter's?
-Yeah.

116
00:04:55,503 --> 00:04:57,714
Yeah! That's amazing.

117
00:05:01,301 --> 00:05:03,678
-[Seth] Oh, man.
-When was the last time you were here?

118
00:05:03,761 --> 00:05:05,054
-Like, a week ago.
-A week ago.

119
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:06,472
-Yeah. [laughs]
-Right.

120
00:05:10,226 --> 00:05:12,729
-[Seth] What are you gonna get?
-I would love some coffee.

121
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:14,480
I would also love some coffee, yes.

122
00:05:19,110 --> 00:05:22,113
So, how far down the road 
in stand-up did you go?

123
00:05:22,447 --> 00:05:25,199
I did it from when I was 13 to 18.

124
00:05:25,283 --> 00:05:27,702
-So, I did, like, a solid...
-That is so crazy.

125
00:05:27,785 --> 00:05:31,330
...five years. And I would perform, like,
three or four nights a week sometimes.

126
00:05:31,414 --> 00:05:34,042
Why are you going to school?
How could you do that?

127
00:05:34,125 --> 00:05:36,252
-I didn't graduate from high school.
-Wow.

128
00:05:36,336 --> 00:05:38,588
Give me a bit that you had
that you liked to do.

129
00:05:38,671 --> 00:05:40,298
I had a joke about Krazy Glue.

130
00:05:40,381 --> 00:05:43,301
"What's so crazy about it?"
It was like that. Um...

131
00:05:43,384 --> 00:05:44,218
That's funny.

132
00:05:44,302 --> 00:05:45,928
-It was. I channeled you.
-I like that.

133
00:05:46,012 --> 00:05:48,014
-Why's it so crazy?
-It sticks to stuff really well.

134
00:05:48,097 --> 00:05:49,557
-It's what it should do.
-Yeah.

135
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,517
-The most sane glue there is.
-Oh, is that crazy?

136
00:05:51,601 --> 00:05:53,311
-Yeah. [laughs]
-Sorry.

137
00:05:53,394 --> 00:05:54,854
What were your parents reaction

138
00:05:54,937 --> 00:05:56,898
to the fact that you didn't
graduate high school?

139
00:05:57,482 --> 00:05:58,649
They were okay with it.

140
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:00,026
Who are these people?

141
00:06:00,109 --> 00:06:00,943
[Seth laughs]

142
00:06:01,027 --> 00:06:03,863
When two people fall in love,
the symbol for this love...

143
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:07,909
a little naked man
shooting an arrow through your heart.

144
00:06:09,077 --> 00:06:11,329
Is this the best way to symbolize love?

145
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:12,914
[audience laughter]

146
00:06:12,997 --> 00:06:16,793
"Darling, I love you,
and I want you to mar--

147
00:06:17,251 --> 00:06:18,795
[audience laughter]

148
00:06:24,342 --> 00:06:26,469
-[Seth] New York stresses me out a little.
-Yeah.

149
00:06:26,552 --> 00:06:27,470
I've gotten to learn

150
00:06:27,553 --> 00:06:30,431
it's too much energy or something
like that. I just can't, like...

151
00:06:30,515 --> 00:06:33,726
I find myself just like pacing around
my apartment like a weirdo.

152
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:34,811
And just, like...

153
00:06:34,894 --> 00:06:37,230
-You have an apartment?
-[Seth] In New York, yeah.

154
00:06:37,313 --> 00:06:39,982
-But you don't feel comfortable there?
-No. [laughs]

155
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,234
I like that.

156
00:06:43,945 --> 00:06:46,280
[Jerry] Have you eaten today?
Do you normally eat breakfast?

157
00:06:46,364 --> 00:06:47,907
Do you want to split some french fries?

158
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:49,534
-Sure. 
-Let's have some french fries.

159
00:06:49,617 --> 00:06:50,535
-Yes.
-Yes.

160
00:06:51,077 --> 00:06:52,995
-Thank you so much.
-[woman] You're quite welcome.

161
00:06:54,997 --> 00:06:56,290
[Jerry] Let's talk about...

162
00:06:56,374 --> 00:06:58,835
Only because it'll make us both
very uncomfortable.

163
00:06:58,918 --> 00:06:59,794
Oh, good, let's do it.

164
00:06:59,877 --> 00:07:03,923
Let's talk about that night in Jersey
when we bumped into Cosby.

165
00:07:04,006 --> 00:07:05,550
'Cause I'm pretty sure...

166
00:07:06,134 --> 00:07:07,885
that was the last time I saw him.

167
00:07:07,969 --> 00:07:09,512
That night was

168
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:12,682
a [bleep] comedy award thing.
I don't even know what it was called.

169
00:07:12,765 --> 00:07:14,809
The New York Comedy Awards,
or some [bleep] like that.

170
00:07:14,892 --> 00:07:17,019
And Bill Cosby was getting, like,

171
00:07:17,103 --> 00:07:19,480
-a Lifetime Achievement Award.
-Right.

172
00:07:19,564 --> 00:07:20,481
Or something like that.

173
00:07:20,565 --> 00:07:22,859
And I remember him
sitting in the back of the car...

174
00:07:22,942 --> 00:07:25,403
-Yep!
-...with the, uh, trophy in his lap.

175
00:07:25,486 --> 00:07:27,655
And I remember saying,
"What are you guys doing?"

176
00:07:27,738 --> 00:07:29,073
-Yeah.
-And he says to me,

177
00:07:29,157 --> 00:07:32,243
"Well, I just got this 
Lifetime Achievement Comedy Award

178
00:07:32,326 --> 00:07:34,579
because you turned it down." 
Which I did.

179
00:07:34,662 --> 00:07:35,830
[Seth laughs]

180
00:07:37,748 --> 00:07:40,209
Did you win a lot of awards for the show?

181
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:42,170
We won one Emmy.

182
00:07:42,253 --> 00:07:43,588
That's psychotic.

183
00:07:43,838 --> 00:07:47,467
-No, that's the beauty of the stupidity...
-It's true.

184
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:49,260
-...of the whole thing.
-It is very true.

185
00:07:49,343 --> 00:07:51,304
-[Jerry] Anyway...
-Were you a big fan of his?

186
00:07:51,387 --> 00:07:53,139
-Massive.
-Of his stand-up?

187
00:07:53,890 --> 00:07:54,849
Yeah.

188
00:07:55,516 --> 00:07:58,102
-And talking to him about comedy...
-Yeah.

189
00:07:58,186 --> 00:08:00,438
-...was the greatest thing in the world.
-Yeah.

190
00:08:01,022 --> 00:08:03,483
And he said
some of the nicest things to me...

191
00:08:03,566 --> 00:08:05,735
-Yeah.
-...about what I was doing.

192
00:08:06,319 --> 00:08:07,695
Letterman once told me I was funny.

193
00:08:07,778 --> 00:08:09,947
It was like the greatest thing
that ever happened to me.

194
00:08:10,031 --> 00:08:12,200
-I still think about it all the time.
-Great. Of course.

195
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:14,911
-That's why we don't need awards.
-Yeah.

196
00:08:14,994 --> 00:08:17,163
I always feel like the life is the award.

197
00:08:17,246 --> 00:08:18,664
-100%. Yes.
-That you get to do this.

198
00:08:18,748 --> 00:08:21,834
Why would I need anything on top of that?
That's why it won't accept it.

199
00:08:21,918 --> 00:08:23,294
-Yeah.
-It's embarrassing.

200
00:08:23,377 --> 00:08:25,630
Yeah. It was also a [bleep] award show.
You could tell.

201
00:08:25,713 --> 00:08:27,632
If it was an Oscar,
you wouldn't take it back.

202
00:08:27,715 --> 00:08:30,301
-Yeah, I would.
-[Seth laughs]

203
00:08:30,384 --> 00:08:31,594
Did you ever host those?

204
00:08:32,345 --> 00:08:33,346
Half a time.

205
00:08:33,763 --> 00:08:35,473
One year there was
a bunch of hosts, right?

206
00:08:35,556 --> 00:08:36,515
Yeah, that was me.

207
00:08:37,016 --> 00:08:38,476
And I did a speech about

208
00:08:38,768 --> 00:08:41,270
hosting an award show
is not a job in show business.

209
00:08:41,354 --> 00:08:42,605
[laughs]

210
00:08:42,688 --> 00:08:45,316
Because when you have a job
in show business and you do it well,

211
00:08:45,399 --> 00:08:47,526
-they give you more jobs.
-Yeah, exactly.

212
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:49,695
So, when you host an award show
and you do it well,

213
00:08:49,779 --> 00:08:50,988
you don't get anything.

214
00:08:51,113 --> 00:08:53,032
They ask you to do this again for free.

215
00:08:53,115 --> 00:08:54,116
[Seth laughs]

216
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:55,326
[Seth] Yeah. Bill Cosby, now.

217
00:08:55,409 --> 00:08:57,078
That night was the night Hannibal

218
00:08:57,161 --> 00:08:58,621
-first told me that he...
-Come on!

219
00:08:58,704 --> 00:09:00,331
I swear to God, man.

220
00:09:00,414 --> 00:09:02,959
We were on stage after the award show.

221
00:09:03,417 --> 00:09:06,003
And it was like me and Hannibal talking,

222
00:09:06,087 --> 00:09:08,673
and Bill Cosby was like right there,
talking to someone else.

223
00:09:08,756 --> 00:09:10,299
-Like, Chris Rock or something.
-Yeah.

224
00:09:10,383 --> 00:09:14,220
And I was, like, star-struck,
'cause it's [bleep] Bill Cosby.

225
00:09:14,303 --> 00:09:16,013
And I was, like...
I'm friends with Hannibal.

226
00:09:16,097 --> 00:09:18,933
And I'm like,"Yo, dude.
Like, it's Bill Cosby right there."

227
00:09:19,016 --> 00:09:21,852
And he's like, "[bleep] that dude.
That guy's raped tons of women."

228
00:09:21,936 --> 00:09:25,189
I was like, "What?"
And he just like, "Yeah. It's known."

229
00:09:25,273 --> 00:09:28,109
And then, like, a month later
he started telling the joke.

230
00:09:28,484 --> 00:09:29,610
And they caught on.

231
00:09:29,694 --> 00:09:32,113
That's like what kind of made people
come forward.

232
00:09:32,196 --> 00:09:33,698
But, like, I remember.

233
00:09:33,781 --> 00:09:37,451
That night was the first I heard of it 
and being from Hannibal.

234
00:09:38,035 --> 00:09:39,870
So [bleep] weird, man.

235
00:09:40,246 --> 00:09:42,582
-It's like so crazy.
-It really is.

236
00:09:42,665 --> 00:09:44,584
It's like you were on the grassy knoll.

237
00:09:44,667 --> 00:09:47,670
I kind of was. Just was sitting there
twiddling my thumbs. [laughs]

238
00:09:47,753 --> 00:09:49,171
-Yeah.
-Doo, do-do, do-do.

239
00:09:49,255 --> 00:09:51,549
I didn't say anything,
but I was on the grassy knoll.

240
00:09:51,632 --> 00:09:55,136
Exactly. I wasn't paying attention.
I was checking my phone. But I was there.

241
00:10:00,349 --> 00:10:01,267
-[Seth] What else?
-Wow.

242
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:03,060
-[Seth laughs]
-That was intense.

243
00:10:03,144 --> 00:10:04,228
It's intense [bleep].

244
00:10:05,438 --> 00:10:07,356
-Intense conversation.
-Yeah.

245
00:10:08,691 --> 00:10:09,525
Yeah.

246
00:10:13,195 --> 00:10:14,405
Why do you have a beard?

247
00:10:14,739 --> 00:10:17,617
'Cause I realized I could change
the shape of my head.

248
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:18,909
[both laugh]

249
00:10:18,993 --> 00:10:20,411
I think, like, a lot of men,

250
00:10:20,494 --> 00:10:22,496
and I think it's become a trend
because of this,

251
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:25,625
have realized that, like, if you have
a soft chin and a fat head...

252
00:10:25,708 --> 00:10:26,542
[Jerry] Oh, yeah.

253
00:10:26,626 --> 00:10:28,461
...you can really make your head
more angular.

254
00:10:28,544 --> 00:10:29,670
I don't assume,

255
00:10:30,004 --> 00:10:32,882
because I can't see your chin,
that it's a good chin.

256
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:35,926
All I know is a chin
is being kept from me.

257
00:10:36,010 --> 00:10:37,011
Suspicious of a beard.

258
00:10:37,094 --> 00:10:38,346
-Very.
-What are they hiding?

259
00:10:42,183 --> 00:10:44,185
I was thinking,
if I could make another Bee Movie,

260
00:10:44,268 --> 00:10:46,854
-Jeff Bezos would be a big character.
-[Seth laughs]

261
00:10:46,937 --> 00:10:48,564
He'd be the head of the hive.

262
00:10:48,648 --> 00:10:50,650
Yeah, after we made Sausage Party,
we were like,

263
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:53,235
"Oh, we could have characters
named Okra and Kale."

264
00:10:53,319 --> 00:10:55,613
Right. That is heartbreaking.

265
00:10:55,696 --> 00:10:58,991
It's heart-- It's truly
one of my greatest comedic regrets.

266
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:01,786
I was with Will Ferrell recently,
and he was telling me a story

267
00:11:01,869 --> 00:11:05,331
about how he was at an award show
years, and years, and years ago,

268
00:11:05,414 --> 00:11:07,375
and Prince won an award.

269
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:10,086
And Prince wasn't there to accept it.

270
00:11:10,169 --> 00:11:13,005
And they didn't realize that. Like, they
thought Prince was gonna come up.

271
00:11:13,089 --> 00:11:14,924
-Right.
-And there was a moment when

272
00:11:15,007 --> 00:11:16,842
people didn't quite know where Prince was,

273
00:11:16,926 --> 00:11:19,970
and Will was, like,
"In that moment," he's like,

274
00:11:20,054 --> 00:11:24,517
"I had the idea to get up on stage
and accept the award as Prince."

275
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:26,560
-Right.
-And not make a joke about it.

276
00:11:26,644 --> 00:11:30,439
Just literally go up and be like,
"Thank you so much. I appreciate it."

277
00:11:30,898 --> 00:11:33,234
This must've been
ten or twelve years ago.

278
00:11:33,317 --> 00:11:35,319
He was like, "Ah, it would've been
the funniest thing

279
00:11:35,403 --> 00:11:37,029
that ever [bleep] happened." 

280
00:11:37,113 --> 00:11:38,781
-[Seth laughs]
-That hurt so badly.

281
00:11:38,864 --> 00:11:40,116
It hurt so bad.

282
00:11:40,199 --> 00:11:41,784
You know why it hurts even more?

283
00:11:41,867 --> 00:11:44,620
-It's so Will Ferrell.
-Mm-hmm.

284
00:11:44,704 --> 00:11:47,498
-It is a perfect Will Ferrell--
-It's the best joke ever.

285
00:11:47,581 --> 00:11:49,417
-He would've done it perfect.
-He would've.

286
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:51,502
He would not have smirked or laughed.

287
00:11:51,585 --> 00:11:53,629
-They would've played for years and years.
-For years.

288
00:11:53,713 --> 00:11:57,508
Reels on his reel on retrospectives.
It would've been...

289
00:11:59,510 --> 00:12:01,762
-[Jerry] You're doing a test?
-[Seth] I feel good about it.

290
00:12:01,846 --> 00:12:03,222
Do you? I see you sweating.

291
00:12:03,305 --> 00:12:05,182
-I just sweat--
-I see a little sheen.

292
00:12:05,266 --> 00:12:07,685
-You sweat all the time?
-I don't sweat all the time.

293
00:12:07,768 --> 00:12:09,812
-But I'm sweating now. Is it okay?
-Are you nervous?

294
00:12:09,895 --> 00:12:11,230
I'm nervous with you a little bit.

295
00:12:11,313 --> 00:12:13,441
You're pulling the shirt
like you're shvitzing.

296
00:12:13,524 --> 00:12:14,775
I am shvitzing a bit.

297
00:12:15,443 --> 00:12:17,570
A napkin under the shirt.

298
00:12:17,653 --> 00:12:19,447
You made me "nervouser" about it.

299
00:12:19,530 --> 00:12:20,906
So gross, what you're doing.

300
00:12:20,990 --> 00:12:22,908
-In an attempt to be...
-[Seth laughing]

301
00:12:22,992 --> 00:12:24,493
...somehow polite...

302
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:26,704
You're making it so much worse.

303
00:12:26,787 --> 00:12:28,330
[both laughing]

304
00:12:29,081 --> 00:12:31,792
-Why'd you do this to me, Jerry?
-[Jerry] Because it's funny.

305
00:12:31,876 --> 00:12:33,544
[both laughing]

306
00:12:33,627 --> 00:12:35,504
-[Seth] Do I seem nervous?
-Yes.

307
00:12:35,588 --> 00:12:37,798
-Like, don't say that.
-You're talking incessantly.

308
00:12:38,382 --> 00:12:39,550
Don't do this to me, Jerry.

309
00:12:39,633 --> 00:12:40,468
[both laughing]

310
00:12:40,551 --> 00:12:41,761
You [bleep] dick.

311
00:12:41,844 --> 00:12:42,803
[both laughing]

312
00:12:49,059 --> 00:12:52,396
-[Jerry] What is that?
-It's just a cage full of [bleep].

313
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:54,190
-Who would cage garbage?
-[Seth laughs]

314
00:12:54,273 --> 00:12:56,442
With a pad-- With a combination lock?

315
00:12:57,985 --> 00:13:02,907
You're good at being a famous person.
But I find it's a skill that is 100%

316
00:13:03,365 --> 00:13:06,869
not associated with the things
that make people famous.

317
00:13:06,952 --> 00:13:10,414
It's like acting is one skill, and being
a famous person is another skill.

318
00:13:10,498 --> 00:13:11,707
Just 'cause you're a good actor

319
00:13:11,790 --> 00:13:13,793
doesn't mean you'll be good
at being a famous person.

320
00:13:13,876 --> 00:13:15,002
-Know what I mean?
-For sure.

321
00:13:15,085 --> 00:13:18,172
Well, I think maybe stand-up comics,
though, are probably better at it.

322
00:13:18,297 --> 00:13:19,423
-In general.
-They are.

323
00:13:19,507 --> 00:13:20,883
-Again.
-Because they, like--

324
00:13:20,966 --> 00:13:22,676
-The raw interface is what we do.
-Yeah.

325
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,137
They are good with the raw,
and they're good at, like,

326
00:13:25,471 --> 00:13:27,473
managing energies and kind of...

327
00:13:27,556 --> 00:13:30,184
-Right.
-...shifting people into their zone.

328
00:13:30,267 --> 00:13:32,019
-That's right.
-And, like,

329
00:13:32,102 --> 00:13:33,187
-that...
-Agility.

330
00:13:33,270 --> 00:13:35,606
Exactly. And, like,
oh, if someone comes in hot,

331
00:13:35,689 --> 00:13:37,483
-you can, like, lower their energy.
-Yeah.

332
00:13:37,566 --> 00:13:39,360
And if someone is being awkward,

333
00:13:39,443 --> 00:13:40,945
-you can break the ice.
-Yeah.

334
00:13:41,028 --> 00:13:43,614
But, like... and that's a skill
as a comic you need,

335
00:13:43,906 --> 00:13:45,783
but an actor
doesn't need that skill

336
00:13:45,866 --> 00:13:47,701
-at all.
-[Jerry] Right, right.

337
00:13:48,744 --> 00:13:52,540
You know in the song "Love Machine,"
the guy that does that...

338
00:13:52,623 --> 00:13:53,999
"Ooh, yeah."

339
00:13:54,083 --> 00:13:56,043
-Yeah. Ooh, yeah.
-You would be--

340
00:13:56,126 --> 00:13:57,753
-Yeah. You could do that.
-I could do that.

341
00:13:57,837 --> 00:14:00,839
[singing a beat]

342
00:14:00,923 --> 00:14:02,132
Yeah. [laughs]

343
00:14:02,216 --> 00:14:04,260
I find that musicians like comedians.

344
00:14:04,343 --> 00:14:06,470
-I like--
-It's like chimps and orangutans.

345
00:14:06,554 --> 00:14:08,556
-Exactly.
-We know we're similar,

346
00:14:08,639 --> 00:14:10,558
-but we don't really...
-We don't quite get how.

347
00:14:10,641 --> 00:14:13,435
-We can't really... hang out.
-We couldn't put our finger on it.

348
00:14:13,519 --> 00:14:14,353
[both] Yeah.

349
00:14:16,397 --> 00:14:19,024
[Jerry] Here's what I want.
Here's a movement I want to start,

350
00:14:19,108 --> 00:14:21,026
-and I'm gonna ask you to start it.
-I'll do it.

351
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:22,069
I want...

352
00:14:22,862 --> 00:14:25,322
movie posters to stop

353
00:14:25,906 --> 00:14:27,992
having a facial expression

354
00:14:28,075 --> 00:14:31,912
where one person is not aware
of how much they're irritating

355
00:14:31,996 --> 00:14:34,456
-the other person in the poster.
-Other person. [laughs]

356
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:37,376
I probably had three movies
where that is the poster. [laughs]

357
00:14:37,459 --> 00:14:38,669
That's fine. I'm gonna...

358
00:14:38,752 --> 00:14:40,921
-I'm gonna give you pass on all that.
-[Seth] It's okay.

359
00:14:43,215 --> 00:14:45,509
-[Jerry] Hey, wanna go to Arby's?
-[Seth] Yeah, let's do it.

360
00:14:45,593 --> 00:14:47,511
[Jerry] Let's see
if they "have the meats."

361
00:15:03,694 --> 00:15:05,195
Hello, gentlemen. How are you?

362
00:15:06,864 --> 00:15:08,490
Can you... Can you say it?

363
00:15:08,949 --> 00:15:10,367
We have the meats!

364
00:15:11,535 --> 00:15:14,788
Twelve-year-olds' favorite prank call,
to call us and say,

365
00:15:14,872 --> 00:15:16,165
"Do you..." I mean, literally,

366
00:15:16,248 --> 00:15:18,083
-two or three times a day.
-Do you see where...

367
00:15:18,167 --> 00:15:19,877
-That's where we're at.
-We're in good shape.

368
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:21,337
-[man] Jerry.
-Thank you.

369
00:15:21,879 --> 00:15:23,589
-Here you go, you guys.
-Do you have 'em?

370
00:15:23,672 --> 00:15:27,301
Yeah, we have everything. Arby's,
Horsey, Ketchup, Ranch, BBQ, Buffalo,

371
00:15:27,384 --> 00:15:29,053
-Honey Mustard--
-But what else do you have?

372
00:15:29,136 --> 00:15:30,262
We have the meats!

373
00:15:30,346 --> 00:15:31,597
[Jerry laughs]

374
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:33,140
[man] Have a great day, guys.

375
00:15:36,143 --> 00:15:37,102
Thank you.

376
00:15:37,603 --> 00:15:40,022
-Who has the meats now, Arby's?
 -Yeah, who has 'em now?

377
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:55,162
-That was good.
-Wow. That was great.

378
00:15:55,537 --> 00:15:58,123
-You wouldn't expect it to be delicious.
-[Jerry] No. But it was.

379
00:16:07,633 --> 00:16:10,678
"The Song of the Heart," Happy Feet.
Music and lyrics by Prince.

380
00:16:10,761 --> 00:16:11,929
[applause]

